.TH REFSTRESS 1
.SH NAME
refstress \- hammer reference counts from many processes at once
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B refstress
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B Refstress
puts sixteen processes on a single shared heap object and has each of them
assign and drop a reference to it forty thousand times.
.PP
Every pointer assignment in Dis decrements the old target's reference
count and increments the new one.
Those are
.B MacFRP
and
.B MacCOLR
in the compiled path, which until recently emitted a plain load, modify
and store \(em so two processes assigning the same object at the same
moment could lose an update.
A lost decrement leaks, which is harmless and hides the other case; a
lost increment frees an object that is still referenced.
.SS Why it is built the way it is
Three things about it are deliberate and none are obvious.
.PP
The processes share
.I one
object, because the race needs two of them touching the same header at
the same instant.
A per-process working set would run exactly the same instructions and
never collide.
.PP
They do real host I/O in the loop, and that is not incidental.
Dis runs one process at a time per virtual machine slot; extra
.B vmachine
threads only come into existence when a process blocks on the host,
which is what
.B release
spawns them for.
A worker that only spins is executed by one thread together with every
other worker, serially, and cannot race with anything.
Opening
.B /dev/null
periodically is what makes the concurrency real \(em measured at
thirteen processes inside
.B xec
simultaneously.
.PP
It churns the heap before checking.
A lost increment frees an object that the shared array still points at,
and reading freed memory usually returns exactly what was there, so
checking the tag straight away proves nothing.
Allocating hard first makes the pool hand that block out again, and the
tag then reads as whatever the new owner wrote.
